      title: "Ways of Reading the New Testament",
      body: `## Many readers, many questions

The New Testament has been read for nearly two thousand years by believers seeking God's word, by theologians, by historians, by poets, by critics, and by its enemies. Different readers ask different questions of it. This course draws on several approaches, and it helps to name them at the start.

## Historical-critical study

Since the eighteenth century, scholars have studied the New Testament with the methods used for other ancient texts, asking when, where, by whom, and for whom each book was written, and what it meant in its original setting. Within this broad approach several methods developed.

- **Source criticism** asks what sources an author used. Its most famous result is the solution to the "Synoptic problem" that we will study in Lesson II: Matthew and Luke both used Mark, and probably a second source of sayings called **Q**.
- **Form criticism**, developed in Germany in the early twentieth century by Rudolf Bultmann and others, studied the small units of tradition—parables, miracle stories, controversy stories, sayings—and asked how they were used in the life of the early communities before they were written down.
- **Redaction criticism** studies how each evangelist edited his sources, revealing his particular theology and concerns.
- **The quest for the historical Jesus** uses these methods to reconstruct what Jesus actually said and did. We will examine its history and its results in Lesson II.

## Social and literary approaches

From the 1970s, scholars began to ask about the **social world** of the early Christians: their class, their communities, their relations with the surrounding society. Meeks's *The First Urban Christians* is a classic of this approach. Others drew on anthropology, studying honor and shame, purity, and patronage.

**Literary** approaches read the texts as wholes, attending to plot, character, irony, and point of view, as one would read a novel or a drama. Instead of dissecting Mark into sources, a literary critic asks how Mark tells his story: why the disciples so often misunderstand, why Jesus commands silence about his miracles, why the Gospel ends with frightened women at an empty tomb.

## Reading as Scripture

For the churches, the New Testament is Scripture: the inspired witness to Jesus Christ, read in worship and interpreted within a living tradition. Early Christian writers read it alongside the Old Testament, finding in Jesus the fulfillment of prophecy and in the Old Testament figures and events that foreshadowed him. Medieval interpreters distinguished several senses of Scripture: the literal or historical, and the spiritual senses—allegorical, moral, and anagogical (concerning the last things). The Protestant Reformers emphasized the plain sense and the authority of Scripture over tradition. Modern theological interpreters have often tried to combine historical study with reading the Bible as a whole, as the Scripture of a community of faith.

## Reading responsibly

One issue requires special care. Some New Testament passages speak harshly of "the Jews" or of the Pharisees—John's Gospel, for example, often uses "the Jews" for Jesus's opponents, although Jesus and his disciples were Jews themselves; and in Matthew's passion narrative the crowd cries, "His blood be on us, and on our children" (Matthew 27:25). For centuries such passages were used to justify contempt and violence against Jews. After the Holocaust, Christian churches and scholars confronted this history. The Second Vatican Council's declaration *Nostra Aetate* (1965) declared that the death of Christ "cannot be charged against all the Jews, without distinction, then alive, nor against the Jews of today." Historians read these passages as products of a bitter family quarrel between early Christian communities and the synagogues from which they were separating. This course will read them in that light.

## How this course proceeds

The lessons follow a broadly historical order. Lesson II studies the Gospels and the historical Jesus. Lesson III follows the Acts of the Apostles and the first churches. Lesson IV is devoted to Paul and his letters. Lesson V reads Hebrews, the general letters, and Revelation, and asks how these writings came to form a single New Testament. Throughout, quotations are from the King James Version, which is in the public domain, with modern renderings where the older English might mislead.

> **Pause and consider.** Can a historical reading and a theological reading of the same text both be valid? What might each learn from the other?`,
